","la":42.361723,"lo":-71.017355,"d":2171},{"n":"Citizens Bank","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":42.354143,"lo":-71.054372,"d":2173,"h":"Mo-Fr 09:00-16:00"},{"n":"Travelex","g":"essentials","k":"bureau_de_change","la":42.362608,"lo":-71.019287,"d":2181},{"n":"ATM","g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":42.356208,"lo":-71.052788,"d":2181},{"n":"Chase","g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":42.362819,"lo":-71.019372,"d":2199},{"n":"Bank of America","g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":42.352148,"lo":-71.056078,"d":2208,"w":"https://locators.bankofamerica.com/ma/boston/atm-boston-105310.html"},{"n":"WeWork","g":"essentials","k":"wifi","la":42.350228,"lo":-71.056993,"d":2213,"h":"24/7","w":"https://www.wework.com/en-GB/buildings/745-atlantic-ave--boston--MA","ph":"+1-857-217-0936","wifi":1},{"n":"Statue of David Farragut (Boston)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":42.335833,"lo":-71.024,"d":1170,"ds":"A statue of David Farragut by Henry Hudson Kitson is installed in Boston's Marine Park, in the U.S. state of Massachusetts. The sculpture was cast in 1891. It was surveyed by the Smithsonian Institution's \"Save Outdoor Sculpture!\" program in 1997.","wpu":"Statue of David Farragut (Boston)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/David_Farragut_Statue.jpg/960px-David_Farragut_Statue.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Our Lady of Good Voyage (Boston)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":42.353011,"lo":-71.048534,"d":1694,"ds":"Our Lady of Good Voyage, also known as the Seaport Shrine, is a Roman Catholic church located at 51 Seaport Boulevard in the Seaport District of Boston and in the Archdiocese of Boston. The shrine has 250 seats and holds Mass twice daily and three times on Sundays. The original chapel was located a short distance away and was built to serve the fisherman and dockworkers in what was then an industrial neighborhood.","wpu":"Our Lady of Good Voyage (Boston)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Our_Lady_of_Good_Voyage_Boston_Sanctuary.jpg/960px-Our_Lady_of_Good_Voyage_Boston_Sanctuary.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Fort Winthrop","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":42.351944,"lo":-71.011944,"d":1746,"ds":"Fort Winthrop, built in 1808 and named Fort Warren until 1834, was a defensive fortification in Boston Harbor named after John Winthrop, an early governor of the Massachusetts Bay Colony.","wpu":"Fort Winthrop","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ef/FortWinthrop.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Barking Crab","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":42.353889,"lo":-71.048611,"d":1752,"ds":"The Barking Crab is a seafood restaurant in Fort Point, Boston. In 2008, they opened a location in Newport, Rhode Island. It has since closed.","wpu":"Barking Crab","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/The_Barking_Crab_restaurant_on_the_Fan_Pier_across_the_Northern_Bridge_from_downtown_Boston%2C_Massachusetts_LCCN2011632398.tif/lossy-page1-960px-The_Barking_Crab_restaurant_on_the_Fan_Pier_across_the_Northern_Bridge_from_downtown_Boston%2C_Massachusetts_LCCN2011632398.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Fort Independence (Massachusetts)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":42.338056,"lo":-71.011667,"d":1766,"ds":"Fort Independence is a granite bastion fort that provided harbor defenses for Boston, Massachusetts, located on Castle Island. Fort Independence is one of the oldest continuously fortified sites of English origin in the United States. The first primitive fortification was called \"The Castle\", placed on the site in 1634.","wpu":"Fort Independence (Massachusetts)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Fort_independence_mass.jpg/960px-Fort_independence_mass.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"United States lightship Nantucket (LV-112)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":42.361111,"lo":-71.035278,"d":1825,"ds":"United States lightship Nantucket (LV-112) is a National Historic Landmark lightship that served at the Lightship Nantucket position. She was the last serving lightship and at time of its application as a landmark, one of only two capable of moving under their own power. She served as the lightship for such notable vessels as the liners United States, Queen Mary, and Normandie.","wpu":"United States lightship Nantucket (LV-112)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Lightship_Nantucket_%28LV-112%29_in_Boston_harbor_2018_crop.agr.jpg/960px-Lightship_Nantucket_%28LV-112%29_in_Boston_harbor_2018_crop.agr.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Fortification of Dorchester Heights","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":42.332778,"lo":-71.046389,"d":1856,"ds":"The Fortification of Dorchester Heights was a decisive action early in the American Revolutionary War that precipitated the end of the siege of Boston and the withdrawal of British troops from that city.","wpu":"Fortification of Dorchester Heights","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/59/Boston_1775.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Castle Island (Massachusetts)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":42.337598,"lo":-71.010605,"d":1867,"ds":"Castle Island is a peninsula in South Boston on the shore of Boston Harbor. The landmass was an island until 1928, when it was connected to the mainland by a narrow strip of land. Castle Island has been the site of a fortification since 1634, and is currently a recreation site and the location of Fort Independence.","wpu":"Castle Island (Massachusetts)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Fort_Independence_on_Castle_Island.JPG/960px-Fort_Independence_on_Castle_Island.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Saint Augustine Chapel and Cemetery","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":42.334167,"lo":-71.049444,"d":1936,"ds":"Saint Augustine Chapel and Cemetery is a historic Catholic church on Dorchester Street between West Sixth and Tudor Streets in the South Boston neighborhood of Boston, Massachusetts. Built in 1818–19, it is the oldest Catholic church building in Massachusetts; the cemetery, established 1818 is the state's oldest Catholic cemetery.","wpu":"Saint Augustine Chapel and Cemetery","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Saint_Augustine_Chapel_and_Cemetery_South_Boston_MA_01.jpg/960px-Saint_Augustine_Chapel_and_Cemetery_South_Boston_MA_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"South Bay Harbor Trail","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":42.346,"lo":-71.055,"d":1975,"ds":"The South Bay Harbor Trail is a mixed-use path in Boston, Massachusetts opened in 2011 The rail trail extends from Ruggles Station along Melnea Cass Boulevard, across Massachusetts Avenue, along Biosquare Drive near the Massachusetts Avenue Connector, under the Southeast Expressway (Interstate 93), and connects with the Boston Harborwalk in Rolling Bridge Park on Fort Point Channel.","wpu":"South Bay Harbor Trail","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/South_Bay_Harbor_Trail%2C_Boston%2C_2016.jpg/960px-South_Bay_Harbor_Trail%2C_Boston%2C_2016.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Governors Island (Massachusetts)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":42.351944,"lo":-71.007778,"d":2058,"ds":"Governors Island was an island in Boston Harbor in the U.S. state of Massachusetts. The island was subsumed by land reclamation for the construction and extension of Logan International Airport. Governor's Island was originally owned by early Massachusetts founder Roger Conant, and known as Conant's Island.","wpu":"Governors Island (Massachusetts)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Boston_MKL_Bd._3_1890_%28128589392%29.jpg/960px-Boston_MKL_Bd._3_1890_%28128589392%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Federal Reserve Bank Building (Boston)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":42.352778,"lo":-71.054167,"d":2091,"ds":"The Federal Reserve Bank Building is the seventh-tallest building in Boston, Massachusetts. It is located at Dewey Square, on the convergence of Fort Point and the Financial District neighborhoods. In close proximity to the Boston Harbor, the Fort Point Channel and major intermodal South Station terminal, the building is marked by a distinctive opening near ground level which allows sea breezes to pass through.","wpu":"Federal Reserve Bank Building (Boston)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Federal_Reserve_Bank_Building%2C_Boston%2C_Massachusetts.jpg/960px-Federal_Reserve_Bank_Building%2C_Boston%2C_Massachusetts.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Old Harbor Reservation Parkways","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":42.329444,"lo":-71.045833,"d":2116,"ds":"The Old Harbor Reservation Parkways are three historic roads in South Boston. They are part of the Boston parkway system designed by Frederick Law Olmsted. They include all of William J. Day Boulevard, running from Castle Island to Kosciuszko Circle along Pleasure Bay and the Old Harbor shore.","wpu":"Old Harbor Reservation Parkways","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Old_Harbor_Reservation_Parkways.png/960px-Old_Harbor_Reservation_Parkways.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Boston ( BAW-stən, ) is the capital and most populous city of the U.S. state of Massachusetts. It serves as a cultural and financial center of New England, a region of the Northeastern United States. Boston has an area of 48.4 square miles (125 km2) and a population of 675,647 as of the 2020 census, making it the third-most populous city in the Northeastern United States after New York City and Philadelphia.","wpu":"Boston","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/ISH_WC_Boston4.jpg/960px-ISH_WC_Boston4.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}